+### A controlled player's prompts go to the controlling seat (Mindslaver)
+
+`Player.addController` gives a controlled player a controller made by the controlling player's lobby player, so an interactive seat that takes control of a Forge AI seat (Mindslaver) answers through `ManaBrewInteractiveController` under the AI's seat index. `publishAgentPrompt` therefore names the seat that decides, not the seat the question is about: `controllingSeat` follows `getControllingPlayer` up the chain (A controls B who controls C: C's questions go to A, since A makes every decision B would make) and stops at a controller whose original lobby player is not a `ManaBrewInteractiveLobbyPlayer` (a Forge AI cannot answer a prompt, so the question stays with the last interactive seat) or at a player it has already visited (two players controlling each other). `promptedPlayerIndex` follows `decidingPlayerId`: the browser bridge exchanges with the controlling seat, and it is the controlling seat's concede, not the controlled player's, that turns the open prompt into a pass. `MindslaverControlTest` pins one-level, nested, AI-topped and looping control.
+
+### A defender choice names its attacker
+
+`chooseSingleEntityForEffect` over a mixed list (players and permanents) is asked as a `chooseFromSelection` of Forge's `toString` labels. Its title goes out as the presentation's `description`, and when Forge passes an `Attacker` param (`SpellAbilityEffect.addToCombat`, a creature put onto the battlefield attacking, CR 506.3b: Hero of Bladehold's tokens) that creature is the presentation's one `CARD` target, so a client can tell which of two identical tokens a question is for. `AttackDefenderChoiceTest` resolves Hero's real token ability and checks each question names the token that then attacks the chosen defender.
+
